<a name="tdsversion"/><h1>TDS Version</h1>

<p>A TDS client works out how to read the server's answers from the version of the protocol it believes is in use.  Field widths and message layouts vary between versions, so client and server have to agree.</p>

<p>By default, SQL Relay guesses the version from the back-end database's version string.  "SQL Server 2012" and later map to TDS 7.4, "SQL Server 2008 R2" to TDS 7.31, and so on down to "SQL Server 6." at TDS 4.2.  When the version string can't be mapped - which happens with a back-end that isn't Microsoft SQL Server at all, or one whose version string the module doesn't recognize - SQL Relay falls back to whatever version the connecting client itself requests, rather than to a fixed default.  Falling back to an old version instead can hang some clients, because they don't always reconsider the field widths they asked for.</p>

<p>The <i>tdsversion</i> attribute on the listener tag overrides the guess.  It accepts a TDS version number as a plain decimal integer, in the form the protocol uses internally: 420 (TDS 4.2), 500 (5.0), 700 (7.0), 710 (7.1), 711 (7.1 SP1), 720 (7.2), 730 (7.3), 731 (7.31), or 740 (7.4).  Leaving it out, or setting it to 0, means auto-detect.</p>

<blockquote>
<!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight 3.1.9
by Lorenzo Bettini
http://www.lorenzobettini.it
http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
<pre><tt><b><font color="#000080">&lt;?xml</font></b> <font color="#009900">version</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1.0"</font><b><font color="#000080">?&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instances&gt;</font></b>
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instance</font></b> <font color="#009900">id</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"example"</font> <font color="#009900">dbase</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"odbc"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;listeners&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;listener</font></b> <font color="#009900">protocol</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"tds"</font> <font color="#009900">port</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1433"</font> <font color="#009900">tdsversion</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"740"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/listeners&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;connections&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;connection</font></b> <font color="#009900">string</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"dsn=mssqlodbc;user=example;password=example;mars=yes"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/connections&gt;</font></b>
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instance&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instances&gt;</font></b>
</tt></pre>

</blockquote>
<p>The version matters for more than field widths.  <b>Clients such as FreeTDS only send a PRELOGIN packet - the packet where encryption is negotiated - when using TDS 7.1 or later.</b>  Below that, the client skips straight to the login packet, and any encryption settings configured on the client are silently never applied.  That is a property of the client, not of SQL Relay.</p>

<p>So if the back-end's version string can't be mapped, and the client's own default version is low, no PRELOGIN negotiation happens at all.  Setting <i>tdsversion</i> to 710 or higher is what makes it happen.  See <a href="tdsprototls.html">TLS/SSL Encryption and Authentication</a>.</p>

